00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:19.800
- I feel very harassed
yeah, and belittled.

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:22.080
- Were you ostracized? Or
was there someone that you-

00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:24.990
- I think on my end, it was myself.

00:16:24.990 --> 00:16:26.340
I understand people have different beliefs

00:16:26.340 --> 00:16:27.180
and I respect that,

00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:30.150
and I think it's good for
everyone to share their beliefs.

00:16:30.150 --> 00:16:31.500
That's the way we progress.

00:16:31.500 --> 00:16:33.690
But I think when people become ignorant,

00:16:33.690 --> 00:16:36.270
or offensive consistently,
without any type of evidence,

00:16:36.270 --> 00:16:37.710
or any type of backup behind that,

00:16:37.710 --> 00:16:38.587
that's the point where I say,

00:16:38.587 --> 00:16:40.710
"You know what? I probably
shouldn't be friends with you,

00:16:40.710 --> 00:16:42.780
because you may smile in my face,

00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:45.687
but behind my back, you
have a different sentiment."

00:16:47.430 --> 00:16:50.070
- I haven't lost any
relationships, because me

00:16:50.070 --> 00:16:52.110
and my friends don't
really dive into politics.

00:16:52.110 --> 00:16:54.090
We kind of just play video games,

00:16:54.090 --> 00:16:55.830
and talk about comics together.

00:16:55.830 --> 00:16:58.380
I think if you lose a
relationship over politics,

00:16:58.380 --> 00:16:59.550
that's kind of sad.

00:16:59.550 --> 00:17:01.830
- Unfortunately, it's
a part of growing up.

00:17:01.830 --> 00:17:03.360
- Yeah. For sure.

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:04.770
- Hopefully you won't lose any friends,

00:17:04.770 --> 00:17:06.270
but usually as you get older,

00:17:06.270 --> 00:17:08.010
start to see those
divides a little clearer.

00:17:08.010 --> 00:17:09.270
- I mean, you guys are older than us,

00:17:09.270 --> 00:17:11.880
was it like this, going
back, now 20 years ago?

00:17:11.880 --> 00:17:13.350
- Politics has changed so much,

00:17:13.350 --> 00:17:15.270
and I think you touched on it,

00:17:15.270 --> 00:17:17.280
that now it's being associated,

00:17:17.280 --> 00:17:19.860
policy is being associated with ethics.

00:17:19.860 --> 00:17:21.600
I haven't lost any friendships

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:25.080
or relationships because I
won't allow that to happen.

00:17:25.080 --> 00:17:29.400
I believe that the majority
of us, probably about 80%,

00:17:29.400 --> 00:17:32.250
are in the middle with varying degrees.

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:35.460
And there are those people who
are very, very conservative,

00:17:35.460 --> 00:17:38.280
very, very ultraliberal,
who are the outliers.

00:17:38.280 --> 00:17:39.390
- Right, I think it's subtle.

00:17:39.390 --> 00:17:41.370
Where you have somebody that you meet with

00:17:41.370 --> 00:17:42.330
on a regular basis.

00:17:42.330 --> 00:17:44.100
You talk with them, you have a good time.

00:17:44.100 --> 00:17:45.840
If topics get brought up,

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:49.230
and it's a, "Oh, okay.
You're one of those."

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:50.670
They don't tell you that to your face,

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:52.020
but it's kind of subtle.

00:17:52.020 --> 00:17:55.380
- And also I think with the
prevalence of social media,

00:17:55.380 --> 00:17:57.480
people's opinions are
a lot more out there.

00:17:57.480 --> 00:18:00.090
- I never understood why anyone
posted political opinions

00:18:00.090 --> 00:18:01.050
on social media?

00:18:01.050 --> 00:18:02.220
I think it's just annoying.

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:03.660
- Well I have three kids.

00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:05.460
They know more about politics

00:18:05.460 --> 00:18:08.310
than I did when I was voting age.

00:18:08.310 --> 00:18:10.110
- It's annoying, yeah.

00:18:10.110 --> 00:18:11.790
- I think it's very encouraging,

00:18:11.790 --> 00:18:14.310
because they're gonna be more informed

00:18:14.310 --> 00:18:16.170
when they are a voting age.

00:18:16.170 --> 00:18:19.410
- I mean, yeah, it just
makes life more divisive.

00:18:19.410 --> 00:18:21.180
There's more arguments and stuff.

00:18:21.180 --> 00:18:23.130
And more, and more relationships ending

00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:25.330
because politics is
such a big part of life.

00:18:27.720 --> 00:18:29.220
- Hi, my name is Scott.

00:18:29.220 --> 00:18:32.880
I am a liberal parent.

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:34.710
I have many children, I have four.

00:18:34.710 --> 00:18:37.890
My oldest daughter is extremely left wing.

00:18:37.890 --> 00:18:40.980
My two middle daughters are roughly right

00:18:40.980 --> 00:18:43.830
in the middle there, and
then Chase is conservative.

00:18:43.830 --> 00:18:44.820
- My name is Chase.

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:47.970
I'm 19 years old, and I'm on
the conservative teen side.

00:18:47.970 --> 00:18:50.460
My dad, Scott, we talk about politics,

00:18:50.460 --> 00:18:54.840
mostly gun rights, and the
government, and the economy.

00:18:54.840 --> 00:18:58.050
We disagree, but we like
hearing each other's side.

00:18:58.050 --> 00:19:01.113
- [Narrator] The feminist
movement is overrated.

00:19:06.030 --> 00:19:06.863
- Oh yes.

00:19:08.520 --> 00:19:12.750
- The reason I sat down,
is because feminism,

00:19:12.750 --> 00:19:16.320
as we know it in social media,

00:19:16.320 --> 00:19:21.320
and the marches, is
generally run by white women.

00:19:21.990 --> 00:19:25.239
And we don't have a lot
of intersectionality.

00:19:25.239 --> 00:19:26.430
Margaret Sanger.
- Margaret Sanger.

00:19:26.430 --> 00:19:27.780
- Yeah, and-

00:19:27.780 --> 00:19:29.460
- Susan B. Anthony.
- Susan B. Anthony,

00:19:29.460 --> 00:19:30.360
total racist.

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:34.650
The women that headed up
the suffragette movement,

00:19:34.650 --> 00:19:36.870
they were not inclusive.
- Of course, yes.

00:19:36.870 --> 00:19:39.585
- And they wanted rights for white women.

00:19:39.585 --> 00:19:40.950
- That's true.
- And that thread

00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:45.950
has followed us, to be very
exclusionary to black women,

00:19:46.140 --> 00:19:49.170
disabled women, women of
color, indigenous women.

00:19:49.170 --> 00:19:51.030
- I don't think that feminism in America

00:19:51.030 --> 00:19:52.590
is based off racism.

00:19:52.590 --> 00:19:54.330
The reason why there's
more white feminists

00:19:54.330 --> 00:19:56.820
is because there's a higher
white population in America.

00:19:56.820 --> 00:19:59.430
So generally there's gonna
be more white feminists,

00:19:59.430 --> 00:20:00.867
because of that.
- Hmm.

00:20:00.867 --> 00:20:03.540
- I think that definitely in
the beginning of feminism,

00:20:03.540 --> 00:20:05.850
feminists wanted votes for women.

00:20:05.850 --> 00:20:07.470
That women would be allowed to work,

00:20:07.470 --> 00:20:09.600
would be allowed to go to
universities, and such.

00:20:09.600 --> 00:20:10.620
I definitely agree with that,

00:20:10.620 --> 00:20:12.270
but I think that feminism has gone too far

00:20:12.270 --> 00:20:14.490
into saying, "My body, my choice."

00:20:14.490 --> 00:20:18.600
And saying that women
are not at all equal.

00:20:18.600 --> 00:20:21.690
Saying that women are being
suppressed by white men

00:20:21.690 --> 00:20:22.863
and such things.

00:20:26.910 --> 00:20:27.810
- No.

00:20:27.810 --> 00:20:30.210
We got a long way to go,
there's just not enough done.

00:20:30.210 --> 00:20:32.190
- In America, or just
in the world in general.

00:20:32.190 --> 00:20:34.020
- Well, America for sure.

00:20:34.020 --> 00:20:35.610
The world in general is
a heck of a lot worse

00:20:35.610 --> 00:20:37.470
than America.
- Yeah, I agree with you.

00:20:37.470 --> 00:20:39.390
- I think that women should be able to do

00:20:39.390 --> 00:20:40.650
everything that men can,

00:20:40.650 --> 00:20:42.300
but we also have to protect ourselves.

00:20:42.300 --> 00:20:45.000
So if you wanna be a stay-at-home mom,

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:46.710
and do one of the hardest
jobs on the planet,

00:20:46.710 --> 00:20:48.690
you absolutely should be able to do that.

00:20:48.690 --> 00:20:50.340
But you should also
discuss with your partner

00:20:50.340 --> 00:20:53.130
and put in things into place,
so that you're protected

00:20:53.130 --> 00:20:54.300
if you choose to do that.

00:20:54.300 --> 00:20:55.620
Some women aren't doing that,

00:20:55.620 --> 00:20:57.420
like life insurance and things like that.

00:20:57.420 --> 00:20:59.250
I think part of feminism is being able

00:20:59.250 --> 00:21:01.260
to take care of yourself.

00:21:01.260 --> 00:21:04.233
- [Narrator] Capitalism
is failing our society.

00:21:10.980 --> 00:21:15.980
- This system that just values
production, consumerism,

00:21:17.280 --> 00:21:20.970
production, consumerism, until you die,

00:21:20.970 --> 00:21:22.440
doesn't work for anybody,

00:21:22.440 --> 00:21:26.430
but a very, very small
percentage of billionaires,

00:21:26.430 --> 00:21:30.150
and it's all baloney
that the system is rigged

00:21:30.150 --> 00:21:32.370
to keep workers working,

00:21:32.370 --> 00:21:35.010
and the rich just keep getting richer.

00:21:35.010 --> 00:21:38.670
- Yeah, I think there could
be some good in capitalism,

00:21:38.670 --> 00:21:41.130
which is like the innovation part of it.

00:21:41.130 --> 00:21:45.780
But in general, capitalism
definitely sucks from the poor.

00:21:45.780 --> 00:21:46.740
There's a lot of industries

00:21:46.740 --> 00:21:49.050
that definitely shouldn't
have any capitalism

00:21:49.050 --> 00:21:49.890
involved at all.

00:21:49.890 --> 00:21:51.180
The medical field.

00:21:51.180 --> 00:21:53.940
It's unjust that certain
people get better care

00:21:53.940 --> 00:21:54.840
than other people,

00:21:54.840 --> 00:21:57.000
just because they're more
financially well off.

00:21:57.000 --> 00:21:59.580
Who are they to determine
the value of life?

00:21:59.580 --> 00:22:02.790
The healthcare system creates such profit,

00:22:02.790 --> 00:22:05.310
and that profit is created
off the misery of others,

00:22:05.310 --> 00:22:07.200
of the illness of others,

00:22:07.200 --> 00:22:09.150
including the prison system,

00:22:09.150 --> 00:22:10.980
where you have jails
that are privately owned.

00:22:10.980 --> 00:22:11.970
Because at that point,

00:22:11.970 --> 00:22:15.000
what you have is a hotel, and
you want to fill up the hotel.

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:18.120
Why would there be a business,
that profits off of crime,

00:22:18.120 --> 00:22:19.140
that doesn't make any sense,

00:22:19.140 --> 00:22:21.000
if you put profit ahead of people,

00:22:21.000 --> 00:22:22.923
people are going to suffer, obviously.

00:22:25.650 --> 00:22:30.650
- So you said, only the
billionaire succeed in capitalism,

00:22:30.870 --> 00:22:32.100
and I gotta disagree with that,

00:22:32.100 --> 00:22:34.740
because I'm the CEO of my own company.

00:22:34.740 --> 00:22:37.140
A couple months ago, I
started a jewelry company,

00:22:37.140 --> 00:22:38.760
and I'm not a billionaire,

00:22:38.760 --> 00:22:41.370
but it's been relatively
successful enough,

00:22:41.370 --> 00:22:45.330
I can like make rent, and go
through college a bit, so.

00:22:45.330 --> 00:22:47.910
I think there's people like
me who own a small business,

00:22:47.910 --> 00:22:50.550
who capitalism really benefits.

00:22:50.550 --> 00:22:51.930
- If capitalism isn't the solution,

00:22:51.930 --> 00:22:54.000
then what is the solution?

00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:55.380
- Do you wanna hear it?

00:22:55.380 --> 00:22:57.630
- Socialism?
- Socialism.

00:22:57.630 --> 00:22:58.463
- Problem with democratic socialism,

00:22:58.463 --> 00:23:00.060
I mean, you talk about how capitalism

00:23:00.060 --> 00:23:01.170
hurts the middle class,

00:23:01.170 --> 00:23:03.900
but in reality if democratic
socialism was tried,

00:23:03.900 --> 00:23:05.760
the middle class are gonna
be paying way more taxes,

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:06.810
not just the rich.

00:23:06.810 --> 00:23:09.150
You see the rich don't pay
taxes because they don't work.

00:23:09.150 --> 00:23:10.980
They make money off assets.

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:12.510
The middle class, they work.

00:23:12.510 --> 00:23:13.680
And because they're working,

00:23:13.680 --> 00:23:15.240
they're gonna be paying more taxes,

00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:17.520
under a proposal like Bernie Sanders,

00:23:17.520 --> 00:23:20.640
or I don't know, AOC or Ilhan Omar.

00:23:20.640 --> 00:23:23.640
- I personally am okay
with paying higher taxes.

00:23:23.640 --> 00:23:25.830
I would love people who
make billions of dollars

00:23:25.830 --> 00:23:27.930
to pay their fair share of taxes.

00:23:27.930 --> 00:23:29.700
But increased taxes,

00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:32.940
for a better situation for people overall.

00:23:32.940 --> 00:23:35.100
That is exactly what I stand for.

00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:38.670
- I'm not sure that
socialism is the answer.

00:23:38.670 --> 00:23:41.130
Yugoslavia before World War II did okay,

00:23:41.130 --> 00:23:42.600
but they didn't do all that great,

00:23:42.600 --> 00:23:44.850
and they were taken over quite quickly.

00:23:44.850 --> 00:23:47.280
What I'm thinking is capitalism,

00:23:47.280 --> 00:23:51.090
although is not the best solution,

00:23:51.090 --> 00:23:54.060
is one of the best solutions
for a democratic society.

00:23:54.060 --> 00:23:54.893
- Think about socialism,

00:23:54.893 --> 00:23:56.490
you think about wealth distribution.

00:23:56.490 --> 00:23:59.580
And then money becomes
a very big role too,

00:23:59.580 --> 00:24:01.530
because the ones that have less money

00:24:01.530 --> 00:24:03.660
become envious of those
that have more money.

00:24:03.660 --> 00:24:06.090
And then money, it becomes a God,

00:24:06.090 --> 00:24:08.640
because you don't want anyone
else to have that money,

00:24:08.640 --> 00:24:09.473
but yourself.

00:24:09.473 --> 00:24:12.060
And if you, for example, look
at the Scandinavian countries,

00:24:12.060 --> 00:24:14.100
they have a capitalistic market,

00:24:14.100 --> 00:24:16.350
but they have a socialist
wealth distribution.

00:24:16.350 --> 00:24:17.640
And that's why it works over there.

00:24:17.640 --> 00:24:21.252
And I personally come from
Europe, so I have seen that.

00:24:21.252 --> 00:24:22.950
- So you think that works?

00:24:22.950 --> 00:24:23.970
- I personally don't think that works.

00:24:23.970 --> 00:24:25.710
I think it takes a lot
of money from the rich,

00:24:25.710 --> 00:24:27.010
and from the middle class.

00:24:29.880 --> 00:24:32.460
- My name is Nathan, I'm 18 years old.

00:24:32.460 --> 00:24:33.960
I would like to ask the other side,

00:24:33.960 --> 00:24:36.180
if they believe America
is the greatest country

00:24:36.180 --> 00:24:37.200
in the world.

00:24:37.200 --> 00:24:38.100
If they disagree with that,

00:24:38.100 --> 00:24:39.780
I want to know what country they believe

00:24:39.780 --> 00:24:40.830
is better than America.

00:24:40.830 --> 00:24:43.350
I do believe it's a blessing
and it's a privilege,

00:24:43.350 --> 00:24:45.480
to live in a country that
gives you the liberty

00:24:45.480 --> 00:24:47.850
and freedom, and to
express who you wanna be.

00:24:47.850 --> 00:24:50.373
- [Narrator] The American dream is dead.

00:25:01.620 --> 00:25:05.070
- I gotta say I disagree with
the phrase American dream,

00:25:05.070 --> 00:25:08.250
and American dream for who?

00:25:08.250 --> 00:25:11.190
The native Americans we slaughtered,

00:25:11.190 --> 00:25:13.470
the enslaved people.

00:25:13.470 --> 00:25:17.640
This romantic idea that
there's this dream,

00:25:17.640 --> 00:25:20.730
is just basically
marketing for capitalism.

00:25:20.730 --> 00:25:23.310
It's very well funded propaganda,

00:25:23.310 --> 00:25:27.060
and has it inspired, good acts,

00:25:27.060 --> 00:25:29.310
and good people to do good things?

00:25:29.310 --> 00:25:30.690
Of course, of course.

00:25:30.690 --> 00:25:32.730
Because ultimately, in a large sense,

00:25:32.730 --> 00:25:35.010
humanity is good.

00:25:35.010 --> 00:25:37.473
But this idea of the American dream,

00:25:40.110 --> 00:25:43.770
I think the fantasy of it is
being torn apart a little bit.

00:25:43.770 --> 00:25:46.080
And I think that's actually exciting,

00:25:46.080 --> 00:25:49.650
because it opens up room
for something better,

00:25:49.650 --> 00:25:51.573
something more inclusive to grow.

00:25:54.930 --> 00:25:56.550
- I think the phrase is kind of corny,

00:25:56.550 --> 00:26:00.000
but the idea that someone's
like building themselves up

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:01.230
in capitalism,

00:26:01.230 --> 00:26:03.570
I feel like that I kind
of have done that a bit

00:26:03.570 --> 00:26:05.460
with my business.

00:26:05.460 --> 00:26:07.020
So I don't think it's dead.

00:26:07.020 --> 00:26:08.910
- I do think the American
dream is dying though.

00:26:08.910 --> 00:26:09.750
The American dream now,

00:26:09.750 --> 00:26:11.910
is not the same as it was 30 years ago.

00:26:11.910 --> 00:26:14.700
Have a family, buy a
home, have a great job,

00:26:14.700 --> 00:26:16.740
but now it's very difficult to buy a home.

00:26:16.740 --> 00:26:20.160
Income has not increased
compared to the price of a house.

00:26:20.160 --> 00:26:21.600
I do think it's dying though,

00:26:21.600 --> 00:26:23.760
but I don't think it's completely dead.

00:26:23.760 --> 00:26:25.140
Because there's a lot of immigrants

00:26:25.140 --> 00:26:27.000
who are still trying to come to America.

00:26:27.000 --> 00:26:29.430
America does have the
highest immigrant population,

00:26:29.430 --> 00:26:31.440
14% of our population are immigrants.

00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:33.120
We have a lot of people
on our Southern border,

00:26:33.120 --> 00:26:35.130
and I think they all
want to come to America.

00:26:35.130 --> 00:26:38.100
- Yeah, I think the
American dream still exists,

00:26:38.100 --> 00:26:39.480
and I can speak firsthand,

00:26:39.480 --> 00:26:41.980
because my family came
from a third world country.

00:26:43.170 --> 00:26:44.970
But so does the American nightmare.

00:26:44.970 --> 00:26:46.950
The people suffering, not just here,

00:26:46.950 --> 00:26:49.440
but also around the
world, due to policies,

00:26:49.440 --> 00:26:52.140
due to exploitation, due to colonization,

00:26:52.140 --> 00:26:56.130
due to a lot of issues that are
directly at hand to America.

00:26:56.130 --> 00:26:58.800
So there's a lot of blood
on the United States hand.

00:26:58.800 --> 00:27:01.500
And a lot of people don't
wanna leave their home country.

00:27:01.500 --> 00:27:03.750
But the situations that
have been created there,

00:27:03.750 --> 00:27:05.550
are forcing people to leave.

00:27:05.550 --> 00:27:08.250
Like my mother would've
loved to stay in Mexico,

00:27:08.250 --> 00:27:09.870
but unfortunately, due to poverty,

00:27:09.870 --> 00:27:13.080
which had a lot to do with
NAFTA, and what happened in 1994,

00:27:13.080 --> 00:27:13.980
directly caused

00:27:13.980 --> 00:27:15.360
by the United States.
- Yeah.

00:27:15.360 --> 00:27:17.760
- Issues that created this migration.

00:27:17.760 --> 00:27:21.120
So is it a better opportunity here? Yes.

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:23.280
My people come here, and
we send our money back,

00:27:23.280 --> 00:27:24.570
to our ancestors.

00:27:24.570 --> 00:27:26.550
Would we just like to
stay where we're from?

00:27:26.550 --> 00:27:28.590
Yeah, that would be a much better option.

00:27:28.590 --> 00:27:29.820
- As an immigrant myself,

00:27:29.820 --> 00:27:31.980
I definitely think that the
American dreams still exists.

00:27:31.980 --> 00:27:34.620
We long for the opportunities
that people have here

00:27:34.620 --> 00:27:37.230
in America, for the job
opportunities that people have,

00:27:37.230 --> 00:27:39.030
the innovations that are available,

00:27:39.030 --> 00:27:42.720
in this free market economy,
this capitalist country.

00:27:42.720 --> 00:27:45.270
- So the American dream is
in the eye of the beholder.

00:27:45.270 --> 00:27:47.910
If you're an immigrant, or if
you live here, it's different.

00:27:47.910 --> 00:27:49.080
It's gonna be different.

00:27:49.080 --> 00:27:51.360
- I don't like the idea
of the American dream,

00:27:51.360 --> 00:27:55.200
but I am a person who's still
gonna always believe in hope.

00:27:55.200 --> 00:27:58.170
If you work hard and you take
advantage of opportunities,

00:27:58.170 --> 00:28:00.450
you can make a difference in your life.

00:28:00.450 --> 00:28:03.210
I mean, you're an example
of it, I'm so amazed by you.

00:28:03.210 --> 00:28:06.300
I think it's awesome, what
you're doing at such a young age.

00:28:06.300 --> 00:28:09.000
I am very, very impressed with you three.

00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:12.030
My hope for you is that
you stay open-minded,

00:28:12.030 --> 00:28:14.400
and that you're always
willing to be learners,

00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:15.900
and not just listeners.

00:28:15.900 --> 00:28:17.100
- Yeah.

00:28:17.100 --> 00:28:18.203
- [Narrator] All right, great.

00:28:18.203 --> 00:28:20.786
(all clapping)

00:28:22.933 --> 00:28:24.712
- Thank you for coming today.
- Yeah.

00:28:24.712 --> 00:28:26.640
- Appreciate it.
- Thank you.

00:28:26.640 --> 00:28:28.078
- Be a good lawyer someday Nathan.

00:28:28.078 --> 00:28:33.078
- Thank you. (Nathan laughs)

